---
title: "FrontierJS — the loop"
description: "Scaffold, run, migrate, deploy, watch, extend — the whole developer loop as one surface with one vocabulary."
render: static
publishes: 0
---
<!--
  The loop. Six phases, six terminal transcripts, all prerendered.

  The hand-written version rendered one phase at a time from JavaScript, so the
  transcripts — which are what the page is — appeared in no file. The island
  swaps which phase is showing.
-->
<script>
  import PhasePicker from '../islands/PhasePicker.mesa'

  // The page's own code samples. Mesa reads `{` in markup as an expression, so
  // a <pre> holding one is a block tag to the compiler.
  const S1 = `<span class="o">model Lead {</span><span class="o">  email String @email</span><span class="o">  @@gate("0.4.4.5")</span><span class="o">}</span>`

  const S2 = `<span class="o">&lt;script&gt; … &lt;/script&gt;</span><span class="o">{#each leads.data as l}</span><span class="o">  &lt;tr&gt;{l.name}&lt;/tr&gt;</span><span class="o">{/each}</span>`

  const S3 = `<span class="o">---</span><span class="o">title: reports:weekly</span><span class="o">flags: { since: … }</span><span class="o">---</span><span class="o">&lt;script&gt; … &lt;/script&gt;</span>`

  export let data = null

  const d = data ?? { phases: [], first: null }
</script>

<!-- ═══ 1. The loop ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="loop">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The loop</p>
    <h1>A framework is the easy part.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Most of a developer's life with a stack is not writing the app. It is scaffolding,
      running, migrating, deploying, watching, and eventually extending it — usually
      across six tools that have never heard of each other. FrontierJS treats that whole
      loop as one surface, with one vocabulary, and the same nouns at every stop.
    </p>

    <div class="loop" data-phases role="tablist" aria-label="Lifecycle phases">
      {#each d.phases as p}
        <button class="phase" role="tab" data-id={p.id} aria-selected={p.id === d.first}>
          <span class="n">{p.n}</span><span class="t">{p.t}</span><span class="s">{p.s}</span>
        </button>
      {/each}
    </div>
    <p class="loopback">↻ &nbsp;and extending brings you back to the schema</p>

    <div data-phase-panels>
      {#each d.phases as p}
        <div class="split2 phasepanel" class:is-shown={p.id === d.first} data-id={p.id}>
          <pre class="term">{#each p.term as l}<span class={l.kind}>{@html l.html}</span>{/each}</pre>

          <div class="card">
            <div class="phasehead">
              <h3>{p.t}</h3>
              <span class="badge {p.tone}">{p.badge}</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted say">{p.say}</p>
            <div class="facts">
              {#each p.facts as f}
                <div class="fact"><b>{f.b}</b><span>{f.t}</span></div>
              {/each}
            </div>
            <div class="pkgrow">
              {#each p.pkgs as n}<span class="badge">{n}</span>{/each}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      {/each}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ 2. Basecamp ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="basecamp">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The control plane</p>
    <h2>Basecamp — where you watch your stuff</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Every app above already answers the questions an operator asks. Basecamp is the
      FrontierJS app that asks them, for every app you run — and it is built with
      FrontierJS, which is the point. If the framework cannot comfortably build its own
      control plane, it is not ready for yours.
    </p>

    <div class="grid3" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>The plumbing already exists</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
          These endpoints ship today, on every app, and they are what Basecamp reads —
          it assembles surfaces rather than inventing them.
        </p>
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="fact"><b><code>/health</code></b><span>liveness, from <code>healthPlugin</code></span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b><code>/metrics</code></b><span>plugin-contributed — caravan and conduit register their own providers</span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b><code>/manifest</code></b><span>migration state, from litestone <code>status()</code></span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b><code>/openapi.json</code></b><span>the full API surface, derived</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card planned">
        <h3>What it shows</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0 0 .2rem">
          One place for the things currently spread across a terminal, a dashboard and
          an SSH session.
        </p>
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="fact"><b>Releases</b><span>what is deployed where, and the roll-back button</span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b>Migrations</b><span>schema drift between your file and the live database</span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b>Jobs</b><span>caravan queues, failures, retries, cron</span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b>Audit</b><span><code>@@log(audit)</code> trails, per model</span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b>Egress</b><span>every conduit target — the complete outbound surface</span></div>
          <div class="fact"><b>Tenants</b><span>db-per-tenant registries and their status</span></div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="card planned">
        <h3>Why it is the honest test</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
          Basecamp is multi-tenant, realtime, permission-heavy and admin-shaped — the
          exact application profile this framework claims to be good at. Building it in
          FrontierJS means every rough edge in gates, policies, jobs and channels gets
          found by the people who wrote them, before it is found by you.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm" style="margin:.7rem 0 0; color:var(--ink-mute)">
          The design-system package has already been through this: its first consuming
          app surfaced eight shipped bugs on day one. That is what dogfooding is for.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ 3. Targets ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="targets">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Beyond the browser tab</p>
    <h2>One component set, several places to put it</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      A Mesa component already renders to HTML, to email, to an isolated fragment, or to
      a JS module. The remaining question is not <em>can it render</em> — it is which
      shells the router knows how to build for.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>What it produces</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>spa</strong></td><td>The default Sierra build — file-tree routing, client router</td><td><span class="badge success">shipped</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>static</strong></td><td>Prerendered HTML for routes declaring <code>render: static</code></td><td><span class="badge success">shipped</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>email</strong></td><td>CSS inlined into attributes, plain-text fallback, subject extraction</td><td><span class="badge success">shipped</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>extension</strong></td><td>Browser-extension container — Mesa UI plus a service-worker relay to Junction</td><td><span class="badge warning">jetty, pre-production</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>widget</strong></td><td>Embeddable island for a page you do not own</td><td><span class="badge warning">config shape, no build loop</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>desktop · mobile</strong></td><td>The same routes in a native shell</td><td><span class="badge warning">direction only</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <h3>The structural bet</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
        These should be <em>modes of one build</em>, not separate packages. Today the
        extension container is its own package, and it pays for that with a hand-copied
        resources layer that has already drifted from Sierra's. Folding it onto the
        target axis is what makes desktop and mobile an entry on a list rather than a
        third copy of the same code — and it is tracked as such rather than quietly
        hoped for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ 4. The through-line ═════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="throughline">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The through-line</p>
    <h2>Three file types. That is the whole surface.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The reason the loop feels like one tool is that there is almost nothing to learn
      between its stops. Every artifact you author is one of three things, and each is
      readable by a person who has never seen this framework.
    </p>

    <div class="grid3" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
      <article class="card">
        <h3><code>.lite</code> — the seed</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
          Models, rules, gates, policies, indexes, audit. Everything in the Data realm
          and everything derived from it.
        </p>
        <pre class="term" style="margin-top:.7rem">{@html S1}</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h3><code>.mesa</code> — the component</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
          A page, a component, an email, a widget. Markdown works as source too, so
          content-shaped pages stay content-shaped.
        </p>
        <pre class="term" style="margin-top:.7rem">{@html S2}</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h3><code>.md</code> — the command</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
          A CLI command is a markdown file: frontmatter declares flags and examples, the
          body is the script. Documentation and implementation cannot drift apart,
          because they are the same file.
        </p>
        <pre class="term" style="margin-top:.7rem">{@html S3}</pre>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="margin-top:1.25rem; border-color: var(--color-warning)">
      <h3>Where this is honest about itself</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
        The loop above is real and runs today — 186 commands across 24 groups, a deploy
        pipeline with rollback, a port broker, a studio. Basecamp does not exist yet.
        Desktop and mobile targets do not exist yet. Sierra does not yet derive forms
        from a schema, which is the largest single gap in the framework. All three are
        tracked in the open rather than implied by omission, and the project is alpha:
        the ideas are settled, the surface still moves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<PhasePicker client:load />

<style>
section { scroll-margin-top: 5rem; padding: 4.5rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
section:first-of-type { border-top: 0; padding-top: 3rem; }

h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.6vw, 2.9rem); line-height: 1.06; letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .25em 0 .3em; }
h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.9rem); letter-spacing: -.02em; margin: 0 0 .35em; }
h3 { font-size: .95rem; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
.eyebrow { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
.lede { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 64ch; }
.btn.outlined { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--rule-strong); }

/* ── Terminal ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.term {
  background: var(--surface-sunken); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px;
  padding: 1rem 1.15rem; overflow-x: auto; margin: 0;
  font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
  font-size: .8rem; line-height: 1.8;
}
.term .c { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
.term .c::before { content: '$ '; color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 700; }
.term .o { color: var(--ink-soft); }
.term .k { color: var(--color-success); }
.term .m { color: var(--ink-mute); }
.term span { display: block; white-space: pre; }

/* ── Loop strip ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.loop { display: flex; align-items: stretch; gap: .4rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 2rem 0 1.75rem; }
.phase {
  flex: 1 1 10rem; text-align: left; cursor: pointer; font: inherit;
  background: var(--surface-raised); color: var(--ink-soft);
  border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: .7rem .8rem;
  transition: border-color .15s ease, background .15s ease, color .15s ease;
}
.phase:hover { border-color: var(--rule-strong); color: var(--ink); }
.phase[aria-selected="true"] {
  border-color: var(--color-primary); color: var(--ink);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 9%, transparent);
}
.phase .n { display: block; font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .1em; color: var(--ink-mute); }
.phase .t { display: block; font-weight: 700; font-size: .95rem; margin-top: .1rem; }
.phase .s { display: block; font-size: .74rem; color: var(--ink-mute); margin-top: .15rem; }
.loopback {
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; justify-content: flex-end;
  font-size: .76rem; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: -1.15rem 0 1.75rem;
}

.split2 { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: start; }
@media (min-width: 64rem) { .split2 { grid-template-columns: 1.35fr 1fr; } }

.facts { display: grid; gap: .5rem; margin: .75rem 0 0; }
.fact { display: flex; gap: .6rem; align-items: baseline; font-size: .84rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
.fact b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

.pkgrow { display: flex; gap: .35rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: .8rem; }

.grid3 { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr)); }
.planned { border-color: var(--color-warning); }
.planned h3::after {
  content: 'planned'; margin-left: .5rem; font-size: .64rem; letter-spacing: .08em;
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-warning); font-weight: 700; vertical-align: .1em;
}

/* All six phases are in the file and one is shown. */
.phasepanel { display: none; }
.phasepanel.is-shown { display: grid; }

.phasehead { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: .6rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
.phasehead h3 { margin: 0; }
.say { margin: .4rem 0 .2rem; }
</style>
